Hi @LittleLittleCloud ,

The proposal is strongly focused on solving the problem of an application consuming replies from an IAgent and IStreamingAgent.  It also tries to solve an implementation problem with specific middleware.  I don't have a reference to the source code of the `PrettyPrintMessageMiddleware` to evaluate its implementation or the use case of the consuming application that would like to use similar middleware for both `IAgent` and `IStreamingAgent`

There are other approaches to solving the problems this proposal is trying to resolve.  It would help to see the code that is expected to benefit from the proposal.

Because the final `TextMessage` has been added to the stream. The proposal has a problem around when the final `TextMessage` is constructed.  The proposal does not appear to address when the final `TextMessage` should be constructed. The current implementation creates it at the beginning of the stream when the reply is created.  This means middleware implementations must perform their work twice, for each `TextMessageUpdate` and the final `TextMessage`

It also complicates things and increases the potential for bugs at runtime. With this feature, there is now a combination of factors. The client can choose either `TextMessageUpdate` or `TextMessage`, and middleware may support either `TextMessageUpdate` or `TextMessage` or both.  This information is not known at compile time, and the developer will only discover this at runtime or if they read the source code of all middleware components to verify which object type it supports.

It complicated the client because if they are streaming with the `TextMessageUpdate` why do they need to support the final `TextMessage`?

If they want to use middleware suited for non-streaming, they can wrap the streaming reply into a non-streaming reply.  I'll see if I can prototype the idea.

It has made building streaming middleware more complicated.  A middleware developer for streaming will have to support both message types which significantly complicates things when building streaming logic.  That means state that may evolve during the stream will have to be handled differently for the `TextMessage` because the logic will have to start at the beginning again to reprocess the `TextMessage` 

Let's look at middleware that will uppercase the response stream. The following code demonstrates the use of the middleware and two ways an end user may consume the streams. The first is processing the stream and using `Update` to build a complete message. The second is discarding `TextMessageUpdate` until it gets the `TextMessage` from the stream.

The two approaches will get different results from the `ToUpperMiddleware` added to the streaming agent.
The code will check if the final `TextMessage` contains all uppercase text.  The first one will pass because the `TextMessageUpdate` will contain the uppercase text.  The second will fail because the final `TextMessage` will not contain the uppercase text.

The `ToUpperMiddleware` could solve the problem by performing the work twice. Execute `ToUpper` on both `TextMessageUpdate` and `TextMessage` types.

Putting the generation of the final `TextMessage` at the end of the stream may not be as elegant.  Code that consumes the client stream using the `TextMessageUpdate` will not observe the `TextMessage`.  Any middleware that only processes the `TextMessage` will not work with a stream that builds the message at the end.



```
        public static async Task StreamingMiddleware(string modelName)
